sections.body:
`@Value` injects a single resolved property directly into a field, constructor parameter, or method parameter, using a `${...}` placeholder that Spring resolves against the `Environment`:

```java
@Component
public class NotificationClient {

    @Value("${app.notifications.retry-count:3}")
    private int retryCount;

    @Value("${app.notifications.channel}")
    private String channel;
}
```

`${app.notifications.retry-count:3}` resolves `app.notifications.retry-count` through the Environment, using `3` as a fallback if that key isn't defined anywhere across any registered property source. `${app.notifications.channel}`, with no default supplied, behaves differently: if that key is missing from every property source, `@Value` doesn't inject `null`. Startup fails with `IllegalArgumentException: Could not resolve placeholder 'app.notifications.channel'` (since Spring 6.2 the class is `PlaceholderResolutionException`, a subtype), wrapped in a `BeanCreationException`. That suits a required property.

The weakness of `@Value` shows up when a default IS supplied. `@Value("${app.notifications.channel:email}")` falls back to `"email"` whenever the property key is misspelled in configuration, with no error. Say someone intended to set `app.notifications.channel=sms` in a profile-specific file but typed it as `app.notification.channel` (singular). The application starts cleanly and always uses the default, because as far as Spring is concerned nothing is missing. Nothing in the logs says the intended override never applied.

`@ConfigurationProperties` takes a different approach: rather than pulling in properties one at a time, it binds every property under a given prefix onto the fields of one POJO in a single pass:

```java
@ConfigurationProperties(prefix = "app.notifications")
public class NotificationProperties {
    private int retryCount = 3;
    private String channel;
    // getters and setters
}
```

This alone is not enough to make binding happen. `@ConfigurationProperties` is metadata describing a *binding target*, not a stereotype annotation that registers a bean the way `@Component` does. The class needs to become a Spring bean through one of a few routes: annotate it `@Component` as well (making it discoverable via ordinary component scanning), register it explicitly with `@EnableConfigurationProperties(NotificationProperties.class)` on a `@Configuration` class, or (the most common modern approach) add `@ConfigurationPropertiesScan` to the main application class, which scans for and registers every `@ConfigurationProperties`-annotated class it finds, the same way `@ComponentScan` does for stereotype-annotated classes. Forgetting this step is a common mistake. If another bean asks for the class in its constructor, startup fails with "required a bean of type 'NotificationProperties' that could not be found". If some code instead creates one with `new NotificationProperties()`, there is no error, and every field stays at its Java default because nothing bound it.

Rather than binding via setters onto a mutable object with default field values, `@ConfigurationProperties` supports **constructor binding**, passing the values to the constructor:

```java
@ConfigurationProperties(prefix = "app.notifications")
public class NotificationProperties {

    private final int retryCount;
    private final String channel;

    public NotificationProperties(int retryCount, String channel) {
        this.retryCount = retryCount;
        this.channel = channel;
    }
    // getters only -- no setters, no default field values
}
```

When a class has exactly one constructor, Spring Boot uses it automatically for constructor binding (older Spring Boot versions required an explicit `@ConstructorBinding` annotation on the constructor; a single constructor is enough as of Spring Boot 3). The fields can be `final`, so nothing mutates configuration after startup. Binding does not make values mandatory, though: a missing key arrives as `null` (or `0`/`false` for primitives) unless you add `@DefaultValue("3")` to the parameter or validate, as below. Constructor binding requires registration through `@ConfigurationPropertiesScan` or `@EnableConfigurationProperties`; a class that is also `@Component` is created by ordinary dependency injection instead. A Java `record` works well here and needs no boilerplate.

`@ConfigurationProperties` classes can be annotated `@Validated` and use the same JSR-380 constraint annotations (`@NotBlank`, `@Min`, `@Pattern`, and others) already familiar from validating request DTOs in the Building REST APIs module:

```java
@ConfigurationProperties(prefix = "app.notifications")
@Validated
public class NotificationProperties {

    @NotBlank
    private final String channel;

    @Min(0)
    private final int retryCount;

    public NotificationProperties(@NotBlank String channel, @Min(0) int retryCount) {
        this.channel = channel;
        this.retryCount = retryCount;
    }
}
```

Spring Boot validates the fully-bound object against these constraints at application startup, and refuses to start if any constraint fails. (`spring-boot-starter-validation` must be on the classpath.) `@Value` has no equivalent: to check that a `@Value`-injected number is non-negative, you write that check by hand, for example in a `@PostConstruct` method. With validated properties, a misconfigured deployment fails in the startup log instead of hours later, when the first request needs the bad value.

examples.code:
@Component
public class NotificationClient {

    @Value("${app.notifications.retry-count:3}")
    private int retryCount;

    @Value("${app.notifications.channel}")
    private String channel;   // no default -- REQUIRED
}

// If app.notifications.channel is missing from every property source:
// IllegalArgumentException: Could not resolve placeholder 'app.notifications.channel'
// -- thrown at application startup (as the cause of a BeanCreationException).

// three source files, shown together
@SpringBootApplication
@ConfigurationPropertiesScan
public class DemoApplication {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        SpringApplication.run(DemoApplication.class, args);
    }
}

@ConfigurationProperties(prefix = "app.notifications")
public class NotificationProperties {
    private int retryCount = 3;
    private String channel = "email";

    public int getRetryCount() { return retryCount; }
    public void setRetryCount(int retryCount) { this.retryCount = retryCount; }
    public String getChannel() { return channel; }
    public void setChannel(String channel) { this.channel = channel; }
}

@Service
public class NotificationService {
    private final NotificationProperties properties;

    public NotificationService(NotificationProperties properties) {
        this.properties = properties;
    }
}

@ConfigurationProperties(prefix = "app.notifications")
@Validated
public class NotificationProperties {

    @NotBlank
    private final String channel;

    @Min(0)
    private final int retryCount;

    public NotificationProperties(String channel, int retryCount) {
        this.channel = channel;
        this.retryCount = retryCount;
    }
    // getters only
}

// application.properties:
// app.notifications.channel=
// app.notifications.retry-count=-1
//
// Application fails to start. Abridged report:
// Property: app.notifications.channel      Value: ""    Reason: must not be blank
// Property: app.notifications.retryCount   Value: "-1"  Reason: must be greater than or equal to 0

