sections.body:
`@Profile` conditionally includes or excludes a `@Bean` or `@Component`-annotated class from the application context, based on which profiles are currently active:

```java
public interface Notifier {
    void send(String recipient, String message);
}

@Component
@Profile("dev")
public class LoggingNotifier implements Notifier {
    public void send(String recipient, String message) {
        System.out.println("[DEV] Would notify " + recipient + ": " + message);
    }
}

@Component
@Profile("prod")
public class SesNotifier implements Notifier {
    public void send(String recipient, String message) {
        // ... real AWS SES call ...
    }
}
```

Two implementations are declared, and `@Profile` decides which one is eligible for a given run; code that depends on `Notifier` doesn't know which it received. Running with `dev` active registers only `LoggingNotifier`; running with `prod` active registers only `SesNotifier`. If the active profile matches NEITHER bean's `@Profile` value, and something injects a `Notifier`, the application fails to start with `NoSuchBeanDefinitionException`. That includes running with no profile at all, which is what happens when a new developer presses Run in the IDE; annotating the fallback with `@Profile("!prod")` avoids it. If a deployment mistakenly activates a profile combination where BOTH beans end up eligible at once (covered next), startup fails with `NoUniqueBeanDefinitionException` instead.

examples.code:
public interface Notifier {
    void send(String recipient, String message);
}

@Component
@Profile("dev")
public class LoggingNotifier implements Notifier {
    public void send(String recipient, String message) {
        System.out.println("[DEV] Would notify " + recipient + ": " + message);
    }
}

@Component
@Profile("prod")
public class SesNotifier implements Notifier {
    private final SesClient sesClient;   // AWS SDK for Java 2.x; needs its own @Bean
    public SesNotifier(SesClient sesClient) { this.sesClient = sesClient; }

    public void send(String recipient, String message) {
        // ... real AWS SES call using sesClient ...
    }
}

